<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isocrates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">436–<a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isocrates was an Athenian orator and teacher of rhetoric. Around 392 BCE he opened a school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> that trained statesmen, historians and orators from across the Greek world. In written speeches such as the 'Panegyricus' and 'To Philip' he urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to unite against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, eventually under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isocrates.jpg" alt="Isocrates" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: shakko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isocrates_pushkin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isocrates was born into a prosperous Athenian family shortly before the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>. According to the Suda, a Byzantine encyclopedia, his father Theodorus owned a workshop that made auloi, a type of musical instrument, and his mother was named Heduto. Accounts including his own say that the war wiped out his father's estate and forced him to earn a living.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His career is said to have begun with logography, the writing of courtroom speeches for others, since Athenian citizens had to represent themselves and hired writers instead of lawyers. He had great talent for it and amassed a considerable fortune; A weak voice kept him from speaking in public himself, and he took no direct part in state affairs, but he published pamphlets that influenced opinion. After founding his school he abandoned judicial work and disparaged speech-writing.</p><p>The school took no more than nine pupils at a time, usually for three or four years. Isocrates encouraged them to observe public behaviour in the city and to learn by imitation, with the aim of serving the city. His pupils included Isaeus, Lycurgus, Hypereides, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theopompus">Theopompus</a>, Speusippus and Timotheus, who set up a statue of him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a>. He called his teaching philosophia, meaning the practical study of ethics, politics and public speaking rather than the abstract dialectic that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> marked out as philosophy. He stressed natural ability, training and practice over fixed rules, and fitness for the occasion, kairos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Of 60 orations in his name known in Roman times, 21 survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. His surviving works include the autobiographical Antidosis and the polemic Against the Sophists, which explains his educational principles. In On the Peace, written at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-357-355-bce">Social War</a> in 355 BCE, he asked that both sides be heard fairly.</p><p>The Panegyricus of 380 BCE had called for an all-Greek campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> under Athenian leadership. In 346 BCE he addressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>. He called for a league of Greek cities, not an empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. Reports say he starved himself to death soon after the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE, out of disappointment at the loss of Greek freedom, a report that J. F. Dobson doubts.</p><p>His works were popular in Renaissance Europe, and translations of To Nicocles were given to rulers as a mirror of princes. His influence reached the Roman rhetoricians <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> and Quintilian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isocrates” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Isocrates</b> put the losses at some 20,000 men; modern estimates place them at 50,000 men and 250 ships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its oligarchy gave way in 364 BCE to the tyranny of Clearchus, a former pupil of Plato and <b>Isocrates</b>, and his family.</blockquote><a href="/state/heraclea-pontica">Heraclea Pontica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.72&amp;year=-373" aria-label="Classical Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isocrates"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.72&amp;year=-373">What was here in 373 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…she has brought it about that the name Hellenes suggests no longer a race but an intelligence, and that the title Hellenes is applied rather to those who share our culture than to those who share a common blood.&#8221;</p><footer>Panegyricus, 50 &#183; tr. George Norlin &#183; of Athens &#183; <a href="http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0144:speech=4:section=50" rel="noopener">www.perseus.tufts.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Do not do to others that which angers you when they do it to you.&#8221;</p><footer>Nicocles, or the Cyprians, 61 &#183; tr. George Norlin &#183; written for King Nicocles of Salamis to speak to his subjects &#183; <a href="http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0144:speech=3:section=61" rel="noopener">www.perseus.tufts.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The base honor their friends only when they are present; the good cherish theirs even when they are far away…&#8221;</p><footer>To Demonicus, 1 &#183; tr. George Norlin &#183; from a speech handed down among his works, which some scholars think a pupil's &#183; <a href="http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0144:speech=1:section=1" rel="noopener">www.perseus.tufts.edu</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
